'''''Petrorhagia nanteuilii''''' is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Caryophyllaceae]].<ref name=APNI>{{cite web |url=http://www.anbg.gov.au/cgi-bin/apni?TAXON_NAME=Petrorhagia+nanteuilii |title=''Petrorhagia nanteuilii'' |accessdate=12 June 2012 |work=[[Australian Plant Name Index]] (APNI), IBIS database |publisher=Centre for Plant Biodiversity Research, Australian Government, Canberra }}{{Dead link|date=January 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> Common names include '''childing pink''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> '''productive carnation''', '''proliferous pink''' and '''wild carnation'''.<ref name=GRIN>{{GRIN | accessdate=12 June 2012}}</ref> P. nanteuilii in the British Isles was formerly not reliably separated from [[Petrorhagia prolifera|P. prolifera]] but now the two are recorded as separate species.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.brc.ac.uk/plantatlas/plant/petrorhagia-nanteuilii|title=Petrorhagia nanteuilii &#124; Online Atlas of the British and Irish Flora|access-date=2022-04-15|archive-date=2022-04-15|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220415170408/https://www.brc.ac.uk/plantatlas/plant/petrorhagia-nanteuilii|url-status=dead}}</ref>  Because of this the commonly used name '''proliferous pink''' is probably not correct for this taxon.  It is an [[annual plant|annual]] that grows to 50&nbsp;cm tall. Leaves are linear, opposite and stem clasping. Small pink flowers are produced in small ovoid heads, with usually only one flower visible at a time. These are followed by ovoid seed capsules.<ref name=Viridans>{{cite book |title=Wild Plants of Victoria (database)|year=2009 |publisher=Viridans Biological Databases & Department of Sustainability and Environment}}</ref>

The species is native to Europe but has become naturalised elsewhere.<ref name=GRIN/>

